{
  "raw": {
    "https://holycross.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/Worcester-MA/Transportation-Driver_JREQ-000315-1": {
      "title": "Transportation Driver",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nPart time\n\nAt Holy Cross, we are focused on creating a world-class workforce by attracting and retaining employees dedicated to fostering a culture of professionalism, pride, mutual trust, and respect—one that values appreciation and recognition, empowering each of us to flourish both individually and collectively. We seek candidates who are inspired by our mission and values, and who are eager to make a meaningful difference on our campus. Learn more about Life at Holy Cross here. Are you a reliable driver with a commitment to safety and student success? The College of the Holy Cross is seeking professional, dependable drivers to join our transportation team. In this role, you will operate 7- and 12-passenger vans, ensuring students reach their destinations safely and efficiently. No special licensing required. Job Description Job Summary: Responsible for driving a College passenger van (7 and 12 passenger vehicles) to transport students safely to and from various locations. On call | Per Diem position.    Major Responsibilities: Inspect vehicle before and after trips Drive safely in all weather conditions Follow traffic laws and all regulations Communicate effectively with students, staff, and management Teamwork   Requirements: Valid driver's license with 5 years consecutive experience Comfortable with computer systems/google and adaptable to new software Complete a road assessment with our onsite trainer Ability to work independently Good driving record and ability to pass a drug test  Comfortable driving (Occasional) long distances (Boston/Providence) Most trips are local in Central Massachusettts Fluent English Dependability and Time Management    Shift: Flexible shifts are available 7 days a week across morning, afternoon, and evening hours. Candidates with availability between 9:00 AM and 4:00 PM M-F are highly preferred.   Additional Information: This is an On-Call, Non-Exempt position. The hourly rate for this position is $17.00/hour. Weekly Hours: 0 Annual Work Schedule: (For Seasonal and Temporary Positions, this will be determined by the manager) Application Instructions For all administrative and academic positions, please upload a current resume and cover letter. In your cover letter, please reflect on how your professional and personal values align with the Mission of the College. For all other positions, resume and cover letters are optional, but your most up-to-date work experience and educational background are required. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. However, priority consideration will be given to applications submitted within 30 days of the posting date. Additional Information To review our generous benefits options, please review our detailed information here or check out our Benefits at a Glance . The College also provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities during the hiring process in compliance with law. Please contact Human Resources for questions or to request an accommodation via email at hr@holycross.edu, or by calling 508-793-3391.  The College is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer and complies with all Federal and Massachusetts laws concerning Equal Opportunity in the workplace. The College does not provide immigration sponsorship for staff positions. Candidates must be legally authorized to work in the United States without current or future employer sponsorship."
    },
    "https://holycross.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/Worcester-MA/Workday-Integrations-Administrator_JREQ-000810": {
      "title": "Workday Integrations Administrator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nFull time\n\nAt Holy Cross, we are focused on creating a world-class workforce by attracting and retaining employees dedicated to fostering a culture of professionalism, pride, mutual trust, and respect—one that values appreciation and recognition, empowering each of us to flourish both individually and collectively. We seek candidates who are inspired by our mission and values, and who are eager to make a meaningful difference on our campus. Learn more about Life at Holy Cross here. Job Summary The Workday Integrations Administrator is a key member of the Information Technology Services team, responsible for the design, development, implementation, support, and optimization of Workday integrations across the organization. This role serves as the primary technical resource for Workday integration solutions and provides advanced expertise in Workday Studio, Enterprise Interface Builder (EIB), Cloud Connectors, Workday Orchestrate, and Workday Extend. The Analyst works closely with functional business units, technical teams, third-party vendors, and project stakeholders to ensure reliable, scalable, and secure data integration between Workday and internal and external systems. This individual provides technical guidance, troubleshooting expertise, and ongoing support to help ensure the continued success of the Workday platform. Job Description Major Areas of Responsibility Integration design, development, and support Serve as the primary Information Technology Services resource for Workday integration design, development, testing, deployment, and support. Design, build, test, deploy, and maintain Workday integrations using: Workday Studio Enterprise Interface Builder (EIB) Cloud Connector Workday Extend Workday Orchestrate Develop and support inbound, outbound, and bi-directional integrations between Workday and third-party systems. Configure and maintain file-based, API-based, and web service integrations as needed. Ensure integrations are efficient, reliable, and aligned with business and technical requirements. Help support and write Workday reports. Help support security configuration changes to domain policies and authentication policies. Technical guidance and collaboration Partner with Human Resources, Finance, Payroll, Information Technology Services, and other business stakeholders to gather requirements and translate business needs into effective integration solutions. Collaborate with Workday functional leads, reporting analysts, security administrators, and external vendors to support seamless system connectivity. Provide technical input for new integration requests, system enhancements, and process improvements. Support Workday releases, updates, and adoption of new integration-related features. Contribute to project planning, effort estimation, and implementation timelines for integration-related work. Monitoring, troubleshooting, and compliance Monitor integration performance and identify opportunities for optimization and automation. Troubleshoot integration failures, data issues, and connectivity problems, and coordinate timely resolution. Maintain technical documentation for integration architecture, data mapping, configuration, and support procedures. Support compliance with security, data privacy, audit, and change management standards. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor's degree in information technology, computer science, information systems, or a related field, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Three or more years of experience supporting Workday integrations. Demonstrated expertise in: Workday Studio Enterprise Interface Builders (EIBs) Cloud Connectors Workday Extend Workday Orchestrate Experience developing and supporting complex integrations in a Workday environment. Strong understanding of integration concepts including XML, XSLT, JSON, SOAP, REST, SFTP, APIs, and web services. Experience with integration troubleshooting, error handling, testing, and performance tuning. Strong analytical, problem-solving, and organizational skills. Excellent communication skills, with the ability to work effectively with both technical and non-technical stakeholders. Core Competencies Role-specific Workday integration expertise Technical problem solving Collaboration and partnership Communication skills Project and priority management Documentation and process discipline Continuous improvement mindset College-wide Collaboration: Exchanging information, ideas, and thoughts with others in a manner that is proactive and enhances partnerships Inclusion: Actively engaging with the values of the College by promoting a work environment that embraces diversity and demonstrating respect for the opinions and beliefs of others Innovation: Performing one’s job with creativity and proactivity, improving procedures and problem-solving Mission/Service Orientation: Focusing on service to others and contributing to the College’s collective mission Responsibility: Taking ownership of one’s actions and accountability for one’s tasks and duties. Salary: The salary range for the Workday Integrations Administrator position is $100,000–$108,000 , commensurate with education, experience, and qualifications. Weekly Hours: 40 Annual Work Schedule: 12 month Application Instructions An updated resume and cover letter are required as a part of your internal application. In your cover letter, please reflect on how your professional and personal values align with the Mission of the College of the Holy Cross to form individuals of integrity and purpose who are committed to the flourishing of all. Additional Information To review our generous benefits options, please review our detailed information here or check out our Benefits at a Glance.  The College also provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities during the hiring process in compliance with law. Please contact Human Resources for questions or to request an accommodation via email at hr@holycross.edu, or by calling 508-793-3391.  The College is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer and complies with all Federal and Massachusetts laws concerning Equal Opportunity in the workplace. The College does not provide immigration sponsorship for staff positions. Candidates must be legally authorized to work in the United States without current or future employer sponsorship."
    },
    "https://holycross.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/Worcester-MA/Nurse-Practitioner-Per-Diem_JREQ-000874": {
      "title": "Nurse Practitioner-Per Diem",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nPart time\n\nAt Holy Cross, we are focused on creating a world-class workforce by attracting and retaining employees dedicated to fostering a culture of professionalism, pride, mutual trust, and respect—one that values appreciation and recognition, empowering each of us to flourish both individually and collectively. We seek candidates who are inspired by our mission and values, and who are eager to make a meaningful difference on our campus. Learn more about Life at Holy Cross here. A Nurse Practitioner provides modified primary and acute ambulatory care services to all students. Health information is shared at every visit to the diverse student body to promote healthy habits and behaviors. This role is for a per diem position to support Health Services on an as needed basis. Job Description Job Title: Per Diem Nurse Practitioner Position Type: Temporary / On-Call / As Needed Department: Health Services Position Summary The Nurse Practitioner provides modified primary and acute ambulatory care services to all students. Health information is shared at every visit to the diverse student body to promote healthy habits and behaviors. This role is for a per diem position to support Health Services on an as needed basis. Major Areas of Responsibility ● Provide quality primary and urgent care ● Provide emergency care as indicated ● Provides care under direction and review of supervising physician, in accordance with written prescriptive guidelines and protocols, if less than 3 years of experience. ● Consult and collaborate with supervising physician as needed ● Maintain review mechanism of clinical performance by supervising MD ● Sensitive to diverse and cultural needs of students ● Maintain licensure and certifications. ● Be available to the RN staff for consultation when needed. ● Optimize utilization of existing electronic medical records and prescriptive resources. ● Practice in a financially responsible way. ● Other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications ● Individuals must possess certification as a Massachusetts Adult or Family Nurse Practitioner with a current license to practice in Massachusetts. ● Bachelor of Science degree ● Master of Science degree in Nursing ● State and federal DEA numbers. ● Two to three years of clinical experience in primary care or emergency nursing. ● Current CPR certification Work Conditions Shifts are scheduled on an as-needed basis; hours are not guaranteed. Must be able to adapt to a fast-paced, team-oriented environment. Compensation Hourly rate of $70/hour No guarantee of hours; position is strictly on-call. Weekly Hours: 0 Annual Work Schedule: (For Seasonal and Temporary Positions, this will be determined by the manager) Application Instructions For all administrative and academic positions, please upload a current resume and cover letter. In your cover letter, please reflect on how your professional and personal values align with the Mission of the College. For all other positions, resume and cover letters are optional, but your most up-to-date work experience and educational background are required. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. However, priority consideration will be given to applications submitted within 30 days of the posting date. Additional Information To review our generous benefits options, please review our detailed information here or check out our Benefits at a Glance . The College also provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities during the hiring process in compliance with law. Please contact Human Resources for questions or to request an accommodation via email at hr@holycross.edu, or by calling 508-793-3391.  The College is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer and complies with all Federal and Massachusetts laws concerning Equal Opportunity in the workplace. The College does not provide immigration sponsorship for staff positions. Candidates must be legally authorized to work in the United States without current or future employer sponsorship."
    },
    "https://holycross.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/Worcester-MA/Custodian_JREQ-000867": {
      "title": "Custodian",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nAt Holy Cross, we are focused on creating a world-class workforce by attracting and retaining employees dedicated to fostering a culture of professionalism, pride, mutual trust, and respect—one that values appreciation and recognition, empowering each of us to flourish both individually and collectively. We seek candidates who are inspired by our mission and values, and who are eager to make a meaningful difference on our campus. Learn more about Life at Holy Cross here. The Custodian is responsible for maintaining the overall cleanliness of the College. Instructions to the employee are generally specific; however, there are times the employee must consider different courses of action to complete the task. Job Description Primary job responsibilities Essential Functions include but are not limited to the following:  Empty wastebaskets and trash containers. Clean and sanitize restrooms/bathrooms using established practices and procedure.  Refill restroom dispensers  Clean, dust, and wipe furniture; sweep, mop, polish, or vacuum floors; clean chalk boards and erasers; replace light bulbs. Assist with the setup of facilities for meetings, classrooms, conferences, events, etc. Must have sufficient physical strength and ability to independently and repeatedly lift, move and carry objects weighing up to 50 pounds and to repeatedly lift, move and carry objects weighing more than 75 pounds with assistance.  Strip, clean, buff and apply floor sealer and floor finish to hard surface floors; vacuum, extract and shampoo carpets. Use and maintain assigned power equipment and hand tools; buffers, auto scrubbers,extractors, high pressure washers, high speed buffers and vacuums, brooms, mops, and squeegees for the cleaning and general maintenance of floors, walls, carpets, furniture. Wash walls and equipment; use ladders when required in work assignments. Lock and unlock assigned buildings: secure building when facilities are not in use checking for unlocked doors and windows, report any unauthorized occupants, turn off lights. Follow instructions regarding the use of chemicals and supplies. Use as directed. Perform cleaning and related activities such as removing snow or debris from sidewalks and stairs in areas within ten feet of buildings using hand-operated tools or small power equipment. Move furniture, equipment, supplies and tools frequently. Wash accessible interior and exterior windows. Clean blinds. Launder cleaning rags and dust mops. Have essential capabilities in the following: Interpersonal skills; attention to detail, ability to read, follow, and comprehend directions; communicate effectively both verbally and written. Basic math skills with ability to calculate measurements. Attend to emergencies when necessary. Attend safety meetings and other related meetings. Take classes as required in cleaning methods and techniques, new products. Handle recycling materials. Call in work orders. Assist in the instruction and supervision of student help. Assist with inventory control and security. Safely operate all vehicles and other job related equipment. Ability to perform occasional overtime and weekend work. Performs other duties as assigned.   Education: Required: HS Diploma or GED Experience: Required: Two years of experience as a building attendant or custodial worker. Two years-experience using floor machine such as auto-scrubbers, buffers, burnishes, etc. Other Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Must know how to use and maintain basic housekeeping tools.    Special Job Requirements (such as hours, accuracy, special equipment and/or working conditions): Ability to follow simple oral and written instructions for thoroughness in performing duties. Position may necessitate exposure to outside weather or heat. Good health; physical strength and agility sufficient to perform building cleaning work. Bending, squatting, twisting, and/or crouching are required constantly throughout the day.   The hourly rate for this full time non-exempt position is $17.50/hour. The schedule for this position is Monday - Friday 6:00 am to 2:30 pm Weekly Hours: 40 Annual Work Schedule: (For Seasonal and Temporary Positions, this will be determined by the manager) Application Instructions For all administrative and academic positions, please upload a current resume and cover letter. In your cover letter, please reflect on how your professional and personal values align with the Mission of the College. For all other positions, resume and cover letters are optional, but your most up-to-date work experience and educational background are required. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. However, priority consideration will be given to applications submitted within 30 days of the posting date. Additional Information To review our generous benefits options, please review our detailed information here or check out our Benefits at a Glance . The College also provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities during the hiring process in compliance with law. Please contact Human Resources for questions or to request an accommodation via email at hr@holycross.edu, or by calling 508-793-3391.  The College is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer and complies with all Federal and Massachusetts laws concerning Equal Opportunity in the workplace. The College does not provide immigration sponsorship for staff positions. Candidates must be legally authorized to work in the United States without current or future employer sponsorship."
    },
    "https://holycross.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/Worcester-MA/Director-of-Counseling-Center_JREQ-000869": {
      "title": "Director of Counseling Center",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nAt Holy Cross, we are focused on creating a world-class workforce by attracting and retaining employees dedicated to fostering a culture of professionalism, pride, mutual trust, and respect—one that values appreciation and recognition, empowering each of us to flourish both individually and collectively. We seek candidates who are inspired by our mission and values, and who are eager to make a meaningful difference on our campus. Learn more about Life at Holy Cross here. Reporting directly to the Executive Director of Student Health and Wellbeing, the Director of Counseling and Psychological Services (CAPS) is responsible for the delivery of professional, developmental, preventive, and remedial psychological services within an integrated student health model that reflects the values and traditions of Jesuit Catholic higher education in the College of the Holy Cross' residential liberal arts context. The Director provides clinical, administrative, and strategic leadership for the College's counseling center, which serves the mental health needs of all currently enrolled Holy Cross students. Responsibilities include establishing and maintaining the ethical, legal, and performance standards required for clinical services and appropriate accreditation. The position has responsibility for the overall administration and coordination of CAPS's resources and activities — including clinical service delivery, crisis response, staff supervision and development, budget management, and campus-wide consultation on student mental health — and will ensure coordinated and seamless delivery of care and education to students through robust professional partnerships and collaborations with Student Health Services, Student Accessibility Services, and Student Wellness Education. As a department leader within the Division of Student Development, the Director works collaboratively with students, staff, faculty, and campus partners to promote students' academic success and personal growth by working with colleagues across the division and the College to foster the conditions that enable and shape student learning, formation, flourishing, and success. The Director represents CAPS and student mental health issues to the broader college community. Job Description Major Responsibilities Strategic Leadership (30%) Set priorities and objectives for the promotion of counseling services that support student mental health, wellbeing, and flourishing, including the delivery of professional, developmental, preventive, and remedial psychological services. Lead and oversee the design, implementation, and continual assessment of a coordinated suite of clinical services, therapeutic practices, group offerings, and outreach initiatives aligned with professional standards and in support of the College's mission and strategic priorities. Serve as the clinical leader for mental health within an integrated student health model, partnering with the Directors of Student Health, Student Accessibility Services, and Student Health Promotion to design shared protocols, referral and warm hand-off pathways, coordinated care conferencing, and consistent communication practices that ensure students experience seamless, coordinated care rather than separate services. Align CAPS systems and structures — including scheduling, triage, stepped-care models, and documentation — to support integrated care delivery and equitable access while establishing and maintaining necessary ethical, legal, and performance standards for services. Engage partners beyond the department in meaningful partnerships that support the College's designation as a health-promoting campus, and consult with and advise members of the College community, including Class Deans, faculty, Residence Life, Athletics, and others, on student mental health issues. Using evidence-informed analysis, including outcome monitoring instruments (e.g., CCAPS-34) and national benchmarks established by organizations such as the Association of College Counseling Center Directors (AUCCCD) and the Center for Collegiate Mental Health (CCMH), forecast needs and advance the development, alignment, and coordination of initiatives and interventions to meet institutional priorities. Remain informed of best practices within college counseling and integrated student health by connecting with professional networks, and contribute to improving practice within higher education. Maintain CAPS’ accreditation with International Accreditation of Counseling Services (IACS), including preparation of self-study materials and coordination of site visits. Demonstrate leadership in managed change, including assessing the department, contributing to the development and revision of policies, procedures, systems, and communications, using technological advances to enhance efficiencies, and motivating staff to embrace integrated practice. Prepare and submit CAPS  annual report and present to senior administration and the Board of Trustees as required; participate in College committees, task forces, and policy formation related to student mental health and wellbeing. Supervision, Training, and Staff Development (25%) Supervise CAPS professional staff, including staff psychologists and counselors and administrative staff; indirectly supervise early career professionals and psychology trainees (predoctoral externs). Supervision includes oversight for recruitment, selection, onboarding, training, performance evaluation, retention, and ongoing coaching for professional staff to achieve departmental, divisional, and College priorities. Supervise, coach, and hold accountable both professional and training staff in the practice of integrated care,  including collaborative treatment planning with medical and accessibility colleagues, appropriate information sharing, participation in interdisciplinary case consultation, and shared responsibility for prevention and health promotion outcomes. Provide clinical supervision and training to psychology trainees and staff, either directly or through delegation to qualified licensed staff, and ensure that all clinical supervisors meet licensure and training requirements and that trainee supervision complies with professional training standards and Massachusetts statutes. Facilitate weekly psychologists' meetings, regular staff meetings, weekly staff training seminars, and supervision meetings, reinforcing ethical, multicultural, and clinically sound practice; attend and participate in other College and divisional meetings as appropriate. Design and deliver training curricula that prepare staff and trainees to practice within an integrated, multidisciplinary campus health system and to serve a diverse student body. Demonstrate exceptional supervision and management for staff across identities, skill sets, and experiences; communicate effectively with all direct reports and stakeholders to ensure robust, ongoing engagement and development. Build a cohesive team dynamic and hold team members to the highest professional and ethical standards, including adherence to industry standards for licensing, patient/client care, record keeping, and excellence in all work. In collaboration with the Executive Director and the Director of Strategic Operations, support ongoing professional development to ensure staff maintain licensure, as appropriate, and are prepared to meet students' changing needs, provide an inclusive environment for a diverse student body, and educate effectively in our Jesuit Catholic, liberal arts mission. Clinical Practice, Crisis Intervention, and Emergency Services (25%) Provide direct psychological services, which may include individual psychotherapy, crisis intervention, consultation, and group therapy, and document clinical contacts in adherence with professional, ethical, and legal standards. Serve as a clinical resource for the most complex and high-risk cases, and provide support for crisis intervention and follow-up care, including participation in on-call rotation(s) and supporting after-hours crisis line (e.g., ProtoCall). Serve as the College's liaison to after-hours crisis service (e.g., ProtoCall), maintaining responsibility for contracts and partnership effectiveness. Serve as core member of the campus Threat Assessment Team (TAG), in partnership with the Associate Vice President for Risk & Compliance and the Associate Vice President & Chief of Police. Coordinate with the Department of Public Safety, Health Services, and community hospitals to ensure students receive timely evaluation, treatment, and follow-up care. Contribute to the College's postvention planning and response as consultant and coordinator. In collaboration with the Executive Director for Student Health and Wellbeing and the Associate Vice President/DOS, ensure that emergency response, crisis management, and after-hours response to student mental health concerns is reliable, predictable, robust, and fair; ensure these processes center students and their wellbeing and facilitate high levels of collaboration and consultation across stakeholder departments. Ensure coordination of timely responses to student mental health concerns, and provide timely updates to the Executive Director for Student Health and Wellbeing  and the Vice President on high-level matters, significant health and safety concerns, and barriers to student success and wellbeing. Financial, Operational, and Administrative Management (20%) Ensure that financial practices, operations, and administration advance student mental health, wellbeing, and access in compliance with applicable law and the College's Jesuit Catholic liberal arts mission. Prepare and manage the annual operating budget and ensure fiscal responsibility; allocate resources across clinical staffing, training, professional development, and operations. Develop, implement, and maintain policies, procedures, technology, and systems that serve all students and the College's priorities. Assume responsibility for office technology, including clinical record keeping system, ensuring accurate record-keeping and data stewardship. Oversee CAPS’ outcome monitoring and clinical assessment practices to support data-informed clinical decision-making, and benchmark utilization and staffing against national data sources. Oversee maintenance and administration of clinical space and equipment in coordination with divisional and College partners. Collaborate with stakeholders across campus, including but not limited to Academic Affairs, Athletics, Finance, Facilities, General Counsel, Mission and Ministry, Public Safety, Residence Life, and Risk Management, as well as external partners including community providers, hospitals, and referral networks. Provide written reports, analysis, and documentation, as needed, to divisional and institutional leadership. Ensure that staff and programs maintain appropriate credentials and accreditation with appropriate licensing organizations. In collaboration with the Director of Strategic Operations, support maintenance of essential IT systems and vendor contracts, including the after-hours crisis line and clinical record keeping system. Minimum Requirements Terminal degree (Ph.D. or Psy.D.) in clinical or counseling psychology or counselor education from an American Psychological Association (APA) accredited doctoral program. Completion of an APA-accredited pre-doctoral supervised internship or equivalent supervised clinical training. Current, active licensure (or eligibility for licensure) as a psychologist, or equivalent appropriate license/certification, in the Commonwealth of Massachusetts. Minimum of seven years of post-licensure professional experience in a clinical and/or counseling setting, including at least three years in a supervisory capacity. Graduate-level academic training in diagnosis and assessment, counseling theory and practice, ethics, clinical supervision, diversity, and research methods. Experience delivering or leading mental health services within an integrated, collaborative, or multidisciplinary health care model, and demonstrated ability to build shared clinical practices across professional disciplines. Active engagement with current best practices and an awareness of emerging issues in college student mental health and integrated student health, including treatment modalities commonly used with this population. Evidence of creative, strategic thinking and proven ability to lead in a dynamic environment to facilitate organizational change and to deliver intended outcomes. Demonstrated success recruiting and supervising professional staff, and supervising clinical trainees, in a diverse environment with a critical understanding of clinical and public health issues; demonstrated experience in creating systems and structures that center justice and equity. Experience managing crises effectively and collaboratively. Demonstrated experience in personnel and fiscal management, including the ability to manage a departmental budget, and to hire, cultivate, and retain outstanding talent. Experience hiring, cultivating, supervising, evaluating, and retaining outstanding clinical staff  in a diverse environment with a critical understanding of clinical and public health issues. Demonstrated commitment to inclusive excellence, with the ability to build meaningful relationships and work effectively with students, faculty, staff, and external partners from diverse backgrounds, cultures, and identities. Experience with administrative duties and functions such as strategic planning, developing and implementing policies and procedures, budget planning and managing resources, and report writing. Core Competencies Clinical Skills: Excellent clinical judgment and crisis-management skills, with the ability to manage complex, high-risk cases effectively and compassionately, exercising sound clinical judgment, professionalism, and sensitivity.  Professional Knowledge: Strong understanding of the developmental, emotional, and mental health needs of college students and the application of evidence-based prevention, intervention, and support strategies.  Professional Standards: Knowledge of current best practices, ethical standards, legal requirements, regulatory standards, and emerging trends in higher education mental health, college counseling, and clinical recordkeeping. Collaboration: Ability to build relationships, develop partnerships, and work collaboratively across departments and with external stakeholders to advance student well-being and institutional goals. Inclusion: Actively engaging with the values of the College by promoting a work environment that embraces belonging and demonstrates respect for the opinions and beliefs of others. Innovation: Performing one’s job with creativity and proactivity, improving procedures and problem-solving. Strategic Thinking and Problem Solving: Strong analytical and problem-solving skills, with the ability to gather, interpret, and evaluate data and information from a variety of sources to identify core challenges, inform decision-making, planning, and recommendations. Ability to design lasting solutions that drive long-term goals. Communication: Excellent interpersonal, written, oral, presentation, and public speaking skills, with the ability to communicate effectively with students, faculty, staff, families, and community partners. Mission/Service Orientation: Focusing on service to others and contributing to the College’s collective mission. Personal Responsibility and Integrity: Taking ownership of one’s actions and accountability for one’s tasks and duties. Demonstrated commitment to maintaining the highest standards of personal and professional integrity, ethical practice, and respect for privacy and confidentiality. Supervision, Development, and Team Management: Demonstrated supervisory and leadership experience, including the ability to recruit, develop, mentor, and evaluate staff while fostering a collaborative, inclusive, and high-performing team environment in support the accomplishment of common goals. Salary: The salary range for the Director of Counseling and Psychological Services is $ $128,000-$135,000 c ommensurate with experience & education. Weekly Hours: 40 Annual Work Schedule: Application Instructions An updated resume and cover letter are required as a part of your internal application. In your cover letter, please reflect on how your professional and personal values align with the Mission of the College of the Holy Cross to form individuals of integrity and purpose who are committed to the flourishing of all. Additional Information To review our generous benefits options, please review our detailed information here or check out our Benefits at a Glance.  The College also provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities during the hiring process in compliance with law. Please contact Human Resources for questions or to request an accommodation via email at hr@holycross.edu, or by calling 508-793-3391.  The College is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer and complies with all Federal and Massachusetts laws concerning Equal Opportunity in the workplace. The College does not provide immigration sponsorship for staff positions. Candidates must be legally authorized to work in the United States without current or future employer sponsorship."
    },
    "https://holycross.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/Worcester-MA/Culinary-Assistant_JREQ-000862": {
      "title": "Culinary Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-02\nFull time\n\nAt Holy Cross, we are focused on creating a world-class workforce by attracting and retaining employees dedicated to fostering a culture of professionalism, pride, mutual trust, and respect—one that values appreciation and recognition, empowering each of us to flourish both individually and collectively. We seek candidates who are inspired by our mission and values, and who are eager to make a meaningful difference on our campus. Learn more about Life at Holy Cross here. At Holy Cross Dining, you are part of more than a food-service operation—you are an essential part of the student experience and the broader College community. Our culinary team plays an important role in creating a welcoming, connected, and enjoyable campus experience by providing high-quality meals and exceptional service to students, faculty, staff, and guests. As a Culinary Assistant, you will contribute to an environment where every meal and interaction is an opportunity to make someone feel welcomed, valued, and connected to the Holy Cross community. You will help prepare and serve quality food while supporting a positive dining experience through hospitality, teamwork, inclusion, and service. We are looking for team members who take pride in their work, enjoy working collaboratively, and understand that great hospitality extends beyond the food—it is about the experience we create for every member of our community. Job Description DAYS & HOURS: Holy Cross Dining staff are considered Emergency Essential Personnel and are required to report to work during times of inclement weather. Flexibility is required, and schedules may change based on departmental needs, the academic calendar, campus events, and operational requirements. CURRENT SCHEDULE:  Sunday - Thursday / 12:00 PM – 8:30 PM Perform a variety of duties to support the preparation and service of meals for students, guests, and members of the College community, following established practices, methods, and food safety standards. Responsibilities include serving guests, assisting with food production, stocking and rotating products, and setting up, maintaining, and cleaning food production and service areas in accordance with established sanitation standards. This position contributes to a welcoming and positive dining experience by providing quality food and exceptional service to students and guests. Responsibilities also include safely operating a variety of food service equipment, including slicing machines, grills, fryolators, pizza ovens, stir-fry equipment, and similar tools. MAJOR AREAS OF RESPONSIBILITY FOR THIS POSITION Prepare and cook food items to order, ensuring adherence to established standards and specifications. Follow menu and recipe guidelines precisely to maintain consistency and quality across all food items. Ensure all menu items are consistently prepared, properly portioned, of the highest quality, and served at the correct temperatures. Accurately complete temperature log book charts. Maintain a clean, safe, organized, and fully functional work area that is adequately stocked and properly rotated at all times. Safely and effectively operate food service equipment, including slicing machines, grills, fryolators, pizza ovens, stir-fry equipment, and similar equipment. Supervise on-call staff, student captains/workers, and temporary employees, providing direction and support on routine tasks to ensure smooth and efficient operations. Demonstrate flexibility and adaptability by working in various areas of the servery and dining operation as assigned by management. Provide exceptional customer service by serving guests in a friendly, professional, attentive, and welcoming manner. Contribute to a positive dining experience for students, guests, and members of the College community through quality food, hospitality, and responsive service. Assist with the setup, service, and cleanup of catered events, ensuring all operational and service standards are met. Accurately complete service records and inventory data within the FoodPro system to ensure proper tracking and reporting. Demonstrate proficiency in accommodating students with special dietary restrictions, ensuring their needs are met with care and in accordance with established food safety protocols. Seek guidance from management when questions arise. Understand and apply food waste management practices, utilizing FIFO (First In, First Out) to minimize waste and maintain inventory efficiency. Adhere to all state and local food safety regulations, sanitation standards, and departmental procedures to ensure compliance and uphold the highest standards of food safety. Perform other similar or related duties as required or requested. RESPONSIBILITIES OF ALL HOSPITALITY EMPLOYEES Ensure all documentation is completed accurately and in a timely manner. Follow all chemical handling and safety guidelines according to applicable Safety Data Sheets (SDS). Provide superior customer service and demonstrate strong communication skills to create a welcoming, friendly, and professional environment for all guests. Offer training and support to co-workers, staff, and students where applicable. Follow all verbal and written instructions from managers and supervisors. Have the ability to frequently lift up to 25 pounds, occasionally lift up to 50 pounds, and lift up to 100 pounds with assistance. All staff are considered Emergency Essential Personnel and are required to report to work during times of inclement weather. Maintain schedule flexibility and remain aware of the academic calendar and special event dates. Schedules may be adjusted based on campus events and operational needs. Demonstrate innovation and initiative in performing the responsibilities of the position. Be comfortable sharing suggestions and ideas that support process improvements, operational efficiency, and an enhanced dining experience. Comply with all safety and risk management practices, policies, and procedures. Demonstrate the ability to succeed in a fast-paced and high-pressure environment while maintaining professionalism and attention to detail. Communicate with guests, co-workers, and other members of the College community with professionalism and respect. Demonstrate an understanding of and commitment to the department’s core values in daily work and interactions. Adhere to the uniform standards applicable to the position. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS FOR THIS POSITION Up to two years of related work experience is preferred. High School Diploma or equivalent required. Ability to frequently lift up to 25 pounds, occasionally lift up to 50 pounds, and lift up to 100 pounds with assistance. ServSafe Food Safety Certification or training is required. If training or certification is not current, it must be completed within 3–6 months of the date of hire. Food allergy training is essential. Prior experience preparing meals for individuals with food allergies or special dietary restrictions is preferred. Candidates without prior experience must complete the required training upon hire. Initial training will include a review of relevant information, departmental responsibilities, and the Food Allergy Program. Excellent customer service and communication skills are required to provide a welcoming, professional, and safe environment for students and guests. Upon hire, the following additional training must be completed annually: Title IX Harassment Training and Security Awareness Training. CORE COMPETENCIES Inclusion: Actively engaging with the values of the College by promoting a work environment that embraces diversity and demonstrating respect for the opinions and beliefs of others. Innovation: Performing one’s job with creativity and proactivity, improving procedures and problem-solving. Mission/Service Orientation: Focusing on service to others and contributing to the College’s collective mission. Responsibility: Taking ownership of one’s actions and accountability for one’s tasks and duties. Collaboration: Exchanging information, ideas, and thoughts with others in a manner that is proactive and enhances partnerships. DISCLAIMER The description above represents the most significant duties of this position but does not exclude other occasional work assignments not mentioned, the inclusion of which would be in conformity with the factor degrees assigned. Salary: The hourly rate for the Culinary Assistant position is $18.00 per hour. Weekly Hours: 40 Annual Work Schedule: (For Seasonal and Temporary Positions, this will be determined by the manager) Application Instructions For all administrative and academic positions, please upload a current resume and cover letter. In your cover letter, please reflect on how your professional and personal values align with the Mission of the College. For all other positions, resume and cover letters are optional, but your most up-to-date work experience and educational background are required. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. However, priority consideration will be given to applications submitted within 30 days of the posting date. Additional Information To review our generous benefits options, please review our detailed information here or check out our Benefits at a Glance . The College also provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities during the hiring process in compliance with law. Please contact Human Resources for questions or to request an accommodation via email at hr@holycross.edu, or by calling 508-793-3391.  The College is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer and complies with all Federal and Massachusetts laws concerning Equal Opportunity in the workplace. The College does not provide immigration sponsorship for staff positions. Candidates must be legally authorized to work in the United States without current or future employer sponsorship."
    },
    "https://holycross.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/Worcester-MA/Culinary-Assistant_JREQ-000864-1": {
      "title": "Culinary Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-02\nFull time\n\nAt Holy Cross, we are focused on creating a world-class workforce by attracting and retaining employees dedicated to fostering a culture of professionalism, pride, mutual trust, and respect—one that values appreciation and recognition, empowering each of us to flourish both individually and collectively. We seek candidates who are inspired by our mission and values, and who are eager to make a meaningful difference on our campus. Learn more about Life at Holy Cross here. At Holy Cross Dining, you are part of more than a food-service operation—you are an essential part of the student experience and the broader College community. Our culinary team plays an important role in creating a welcoming, connected, and enjoyable campus experience by providing high-quality meals and exceptional service to students, faculty, staff, and guests. As a Culinary Assistant, you will contribute to an environment where every meal and interaction is an opportunity to make someone feel welcomed, valued, and connected to the Holy Cross community. You will help prepare and serve quality food while supporting a positive dining experience through hospitality, teamwork, inclusion, and service. We are looking for team members who take pride in their work, enjoy working collaboratively, and understand that great hospitality extends beyond the food—it is about the experience we create for every member of our community. Job Description Holy Cross Dining staff are considered Emergency Essential Personnel and are required to report to work during times of inclement weather. Flexibility is required, and schedules may change based on departmental needs, the academic calendar, campus events, and operational requirements.   CURRENT SCHEDULE:  Sunday - Thursday / 12:00 PM – 8:30 PM                                         Perform a variety of duties to support the preparation and service of meals for students, faculty, staff, and guests while contributing to a welcoming, high-quality dining experience. Responsibilities include preparing and serving food, assisting with food production, stocking and rotating products, maintaining clean and organized food production and service areas, and providing exceptional customer service in accordance with established practices, sanitation standards, and food safety requirements. The Culinary Assistant operates a variety of food service equipment, including slicing machines, grills, fryolators, pizza ovens, stir-fry equipment, and similar tools. This position works collaboratively with students, staff, and members of the campus community to ensure efficient daily operations and a positive dining experience. MAJOR AREAS OF RESPONSIBILITY FOR THIS POSITION Prepare and cook food items to order, ensuring adherence to established standards, recipes, and specifications. Follow menu and recipe guidelines precisely to maintain consistency, quality, and presentation across all food items. Ensure all menu items are consistently prepared, properly portioned, of the highest quality, and served at the appropriate temperatures. Accurately complete temperature log book charts. Maintain a clean, safe, organized, and fully functional work area that is adequately stocked and properly rotated. Operate food production and service equipment safely and effectively, including slicing machines, grills, fryolators, pizza ovens, stir-fry equipment, and similar equipment. Supervise on-call staff, student captains/workers, and temporary employees, providing direction and support on routine tasks to promote smooth and efficient operations. Be adaptable and capable of working in various areas of the servery and dining operation as assigned by management. Provide exceptional customer service by serving guests in a friendly, professional, attentive, and welcoming manner. Contribute to a positive dining experience for students, faculty, staff, and guests by demonstrating hospitality, responsiveness, and a commitment to service excellence. Assist with the setup, service, and cleanup of catered events, ensuring all operational and service standards are met. Accurately complete service records and inventory data within the FoodPro system to ensure proper tracking, reporting, and inventory management. Demonstrate proficiency in accommodating students with special dietary restrictions and food allergies, ensuring their needs are addressed with care and attention to established food safety protocols. Seek guidance from management when questions or concerns arise. Understand and apply food waste management practices, including FIFO (First In, First Out), to minimize waste and maintain inventory efficiency. Adhere to all state and local food safety regulations, departmental policies, and established sanitation practices to ensure compliance and uphold the highest standards of food safety. Perform other similar or related duties as required or requested. RESPONSIBILITIES OF ALL HOSPITALITY EMPLOYEES Record all required documentation accurately and in a timely manner. Follow all chemical handling and safety guidelines according to applicable Safety Data Sheets (SDS). Provide superior customer service and demonstrate strong communication skills to create a welcoming, friendly, and professional environment for all guests. Offer training and support to co-workers, staff, and students where applicable. Follow all verbal and written instructions from managers and supervisors. Have the ability to frequently lift up to 25 pounds, occasionally lift up to 50 pounds, and lift up to 100 pounds with assistance. All staff are considered Emergency Essential Personnel and are required to report to work during times of inclement weather. Maintain schedule flexibility and remain aware of the academic calendar, campus events, and special event dates. Schedules may be adjusted based on operational and campus needs. Demonstrate initiative and innovation within the scope of the position. Be comfortable sharing suggestions and ideas that support process improvements, operational efficiency, and an enhanced dining experience. Comply with all safety and risk management practices, policies, and procedures. Demonstrate the ability to succeed in a fast-paced and high-pressure environment while maintaining professionalism and attention to detail. Communicate with guests, co-workers, and other members of the College community with professionalism, courtesy, and respect. Demonstrate an understanding of and commitment to the department’s core values in daily work and interactions. Adhere to the uniform standards applicable to the position. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS FOR THIS POSITION High School Diploma or equivalent required. Up to two years of related work experience preferred. Ability to frequently lift up to 25 pounds, occasionally lift up to 50 pounds, and lift up to 100 pounds with assistance. ServSafe Food Safety Certification or training is required. If certification or training is not current, it must be completed within 3–6 months of the date of hire. Food allergy training is essential. Prior experience preparing meals for individuals with food allergies or special dietary needs is preferred. Candidates without prior experience must complete the required training upon hire. Initial training will include a review of program information, departmental responsibilities, and the Food Allergy Program. Excellent customer service and communication skills are required to provide a welcoming, professional, and safe environment for students, faculty, staff, and guests. Required annual training includes Title IX Harassment Training and Security Awareness Training. Ability to work collaboratively with a diverse group of students, staff, faculty, and guests while maintaining a positive and service-oriented approach.   CORE COMPETENCIES ●       Inclusion: Actively engaging with the values of the College by promoting a work environment that embraces diversity and demonstrating respect for the opinions and beliefs of others. ●       Innovation: Performing one’s job with creativity and proactivity, improving procedures and problem-solving. ●       Mission/Service Orientation: Focusing on service to others and contributing to the College’s collective mission. ●       Responsibility: Taking ownership of one’s actions and accountability for one’s tasks and duties. ●       Collaboration: Exchanging information, ideas, and thoughts with others in a manner that is proactive and enhances partnerships.   DISCLAIMER The description above represents the most significant duties of this position but does not exclude other occasional work assignments not mentioned, the inclusion of which would be in conformity with the factor degrees assigned. Salary: The hourly rate for the Culinary Assistant position is $18.00 per hour. Weekly Hours: 40 Annual Work Schedule: (For Seasonal and Temporary Positions, this will be determined by the manager) Application Instructions For all administrative and academic positions, please upload a current resume and cover letter. In your cover letter, please reflect on how your professional and personal values align with the Mission of the College. For all other positions, resume and cover letters are optional, but your most up-to-date work experience and educational background are required. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. However, priority consideration will be given to applications submitted within 30 days of the posting date. Additional Information To review our generous benefits options, please review our detailed information here or check out our Benefits at a Glance . The College also provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities during the hiring process in compliance with law. Please contact Human Resources for questions or to request an accommodation via email at hr@holycross.edu, or by calling 508-793-3391.  The College is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer and complies with all Federal and Massachusetts laws concerning Equal Opportunity in the workplace. The College does not provide immigration sponsorship for staff positions. Candidates must be legally authorized to work in the United States without current or future employer sponsorship."
