    "https://holycross.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/Worcester-MA/Payables-Accounting-Manager_JREQ-000850": {
      "title": "Payables Accounting Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\nFull time\n\nAt Holy Cross, we are focused on creating a world-class workforce by attracting and retaining employees dedicated to fostering a culture of professionalism, pride, mutual trust, and respect—one that values appreciation and recognition, empowering each of us to flourish both individually and collectively. We seek candidates who are inspired by our mission and values, and who are eager to make a meaningful difference on our campus. Learn more about Life at Holy Cross here. Responsible for providing professional accounting and tax services with a focus on strengthening policies, procedures and internal controls and improving customer service to the college community and external parties. This role works closely with the Assistant Controller providing assistance in the performance of various functions in the financial processing for the college including the supervision of staff with a high volume workload to meet monthly, quarterly and annual deadlines. Assistance is also required with preparation of financial statements, reports, tax returns and other data in compliance with applicable laws, rules, regulations, policies, and higher education industry practices. Job Description Major Areas of Responsibility Manage, supervise and cross-train staff in the areas of accounts payable.  Manage workload to complete work accurately and within established deadlines.  Establish goals and objectives, mentor staff, and provide timely feedback to enhance professional development.  Manage all aspects of the weekly payables process including payments made in various electronic forms and by check. Manage all aspects of the expense reporting processes within Workday, including employee reimbursements processed in various electronic forms. Manage the monthly, quarterly and annual close process related to payables and liability accounts, ensuring that all transactions are reported timely and accurately and general ledger activity is reconciled accurately and timely. Coordinate and process one-off bulk payments for large groups (e.g., stipends, refunds, or event-related disbursements), managing relationships with departments across campus to ensure accurate and timely payment processing. Prepare work papers and gather documentation required for audit engagements and tax returns.  Coordinate with internal staff and external auditors to meet all deadlines. Monitor internal controls and implement improvements to strengthen the effectiveness and efficiency of internal controls. Assist with developing and documenting accounting policies and procedures. Perform accounting research, document conclusions, and assist in the implementation of new accounting rules and IRS regulations. Maintain the integrity of the vendor database working collaboratively with the procurement department. Identify and evaluate opportunities to leverage AI and automation tools to improve the efficiency and accuracy of accounts payable processes, such as invoice processing, data validation, and reporting. Actively participate in special projects as assigned. Projects may include but are not limited to testing system implementations, upgrades, or enhancements, conducting financial analysis and developing ad hoc reports and process improvement initiatives. Other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s degree in Business Administration or Accounting and a minimum of 3 - 5 years’ of progressive responsibility in accounting positions, preferably in a higher education environment. Comprehensive knowledge of accounting standards and regulations related to payables applicable to a higher education institution, including IRS, federal, and state. Demonstrated supervisory experience with a focus on quality customer service and team leadership and mentoring skills. Experience working with ERP (Workday preferred) including electronic invoice and expense management software. Advanced proficiency in Excel, with experience using other Microsoft applications. Disclaimer: The description above represents the most significant duties of this position but does not exclude other occasional work assignments not mentioned, the inclusion of which would be in conformity with the factor degrees assigned. The salary range for this position is $88,000 - $93,000 commensurate with experience and qualifications. Weekly Hours: 40 Annual Work Schedule: Application Instructions An updated resume and cover letter are required as a part of your internal application. In your cover letter, please reflect on how your professional and personal values align with the Mission of the College of the Holy Cross to form individuals of integrity and purpose who are committed to the flourishing of all. Additional Information To review our generous benefits options, please review our detailed information here or check out our Benefits at a Glance.  The College also provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities during the hiring process in compliance with law. Please contact Human Resources for questions or to request an accommodation via email at hr@holycross.edu, or by calling 508-793-3391.  The College is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer and complies with all Federal and Massachusetts laws concerning Equal Opportunity in the workplace."
    },
    "https://holycross.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/Worcester-MA/Assistant-Director-of-Class-Engagement_JREQ-000844": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Class Engagement",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-23\nFull time\n\nAt Holy Cross, we are focused on creating a world-class workforce by attracting and retaining employees dedicated to fostering a culture of professionalism, pride, mutual trust, and respect—one that values appreciation and recognition, empowering each of us to flourish both individually and collectively. We seek candidates who are inspired by our mission and values, and who are eager to make a meaningful difference on our campus. Learn more about Life at Holy Cross here. Entry-level management focused on supporting specific program or project areas. Responsibilities: Executes day-to-day operations within the assigned area, contributes to strategic planning efforts, and assists with project oversight to meet immediate organizational needs.Impact: Plays a critical role in achieving operational efficiencies and supporting the team in meeting short-term goals. (Typically requiring 3-5 years of relevant experience with a Bachelor's degree preferred)\"\"\" Job Description The Assistant Director of Class Engagement plays a critical role in fostering strong connections between alumni and the College. This position is responsible for managing class-based volunteer programs and leading milestone Reunion fundraising campaigns to drive alumni participation and philanthropic support. The Assistant Director works closely with staff colleagues and volunteers to inspire meaningful engagement, cultivate a culture of philanthropy, and achieve participation and revenue goals that support the College’s comprehensive campaign. Major Areas of Responsibility Key Responsibilities Recruit, train, and support alumni class volunteers, with a particular focus on class chairs and Reunion committees.  Oversee 15-20 non-Reunion classes and three milestone classes.   Provide strategic guidance, resources, and ongoing communication to empower volunteers to promote participation and engagement to their classmates.  Support class programming and events.   Design and execute annual Reunion fundraising strategies for select milestone classes.  In coordination with Leadership Annual Giving, identify volunteer leadership, recruit committee members, manage timelines and communications for Reunion gift committees to ensure successful outcomes.    Contribute to the development of class engagement and annual giving strategies aligned with institutional goals.   Serve as the Class Engagement team lead on the College’s texting platform in order to drive participation and engagement across the division.   Monitor progress toward class participation and Reunion fundraising benchmarks; prepare regular reports on outcomes.  Utilize advancement databases and digital tools to track volunteer activity, participation rates, and donor engagement.   Serve as the Class Engagement team lead for Class Communication templates.   Stay informed about best practices in alumni engagement and apply innovative approaches to volunteer management and class fundraising.   Other duties as assigned or necessary. Minimum Qualifications   Bachelor’s degree required, with a strong understanding of and commitment to the mission of the College and the value of a liberal arts education. Two years of successful advancement, fundraising, or related transferable experience preferred/required. Demonstrated ability to build relationships and engage effectively with a diverse range of constituents, colleagues, volunteers, and community members. Strong organizational and attention-to-detail skills, with the ability to manage multiple priorities, meet goals and deadlines, and work independently. Exceptional communication, leadership, and interpersonal skills, with the ability to inspire colleagues and motivate volunteers. Demonstrated initiative, sound judgment, professionalism, and discretion when handling confidential and sensitive information. Positive, approachable, and collaborative demeanor, with the ability to represent the College professionally and build strong relationships with internal and external constituents. Willingness and availability to work occasional evenings and weekends, as required by the needs of the position. Valid driver’s license required. Core Competencies     Collaboration: Exchanging information, ideas, thoughts and feelings thoughtfully with others in a manner that is proactive and enhances partnerships Inclusion: Actively engaging with the values of the College by promoting a work environment that embraces diversity and demonstrating respect for the opinions and beliefs of others Innovation: Performing one’s job with creativity and proactivity, improving procedures and problem-solving Mission/Service Orientation: Focusing on service to others and contributing to the College’s collective mission Responsibility: Taking ownership of one’s actions and accountability for one’s tasks and duties Disclaimer    The description above represents the most significant duties of this position but does not exclude other occasional work assignments not mentioned, the inclusion of which would be in conformity with the factor degrees assigned. Salary Range: The Assistant Director of Class Engagement offers a salary range of $57,000-$60,000, commensurate with education and experience Weekly Hours: 40 Annual Work Schedule: Application Instructions An updated resume and cover letter are required as a part of your internal application. In your cover letter, please reflect on how your professional and personal values align with the Mission of the College of the Holy Cross to form individuals of integrity and purpose who are committed to the flourishing of all. Additional Information To review our generous benefits options, please review our detailed information here or check out our Benefits at a Glance.  The College also provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities during the hiring process in compliance with law. Please contact Human Resources for questions or to request an accommodation via email at hr@holycross.edu, or by calling 508-793-3391.  The College is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer and complies with all Federal and Massachusetts laws concerning Equal Opportunity in the workplace."
    },
    "https://holycross.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/Worcester-MA/Fenwick-Music-Librarian_JREQ-000830": {
      "title": "Fenwick Music Librarian",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21\nFull time\n\nAt Holy Cross, we are focused on creating a world-class workforce by attracting and retaining employees dedicated to fostering a culture of professionalism, pride, mutual trust, and respect—one that values appreciation and recognition, empowering each of us to flourish both individually and collectively. We seek candidates who are inspired by our mission and values, and who are eager to make a meaningful difference on our campus. Learn more about Life at Holy Cross here. Reporting to the Assistant Director and Head of Access & Discovery Services and serving as a branch manager, the Fenwick Music Librarian has a dual role. First, as the liaison to the Music Department, the librarian oversees all aspects of the Music Library and its collections, providing comprehensive research and instructional support to students and faculty. Second, the librarian serves as the full liaison to the Department of Theater and Dance, supporting collection development, research, and instruction in that discipline. The Fenwick Music Librarian will have a breadth of music knowledge to support the diverse fields that engage faculty and students’ research and pedagogical interests. Will supervise one library assistant and a team of student workers within the Fenwick Music Library and serves as the primary developer of the music collection. Provides research expertise, offers regular personal research sessions, and provides library instruction. Manage print and digital acquisitions in support of liaison departments. Serves as an integrated member of the music department community, attending regular departmental meetings as well as other departmental events. Maintains close communication with all liaison departments. Pursues professional development and stay abreast of best practices in the field. Leads, and serves on, library committees and teams. Job Description Major Areas of Responsibility Manage the Fenwick Music Library and its operations Manage the Music Library Assistant and student workers Support faculty and student research, and provide research sessions and information literacy instruction sessions for music, theatre and dance, with a particular expertise in music Build and manage the collections of the Fenwick Music Library and the collections in support of Theatre and Dance ensuring alignment with academic, library, and institutional goals; including management of budget allocations in support of liaison departments. Supervise the cataloging of, and/or is capable of original cataloging of all music materials and formats Gather and report research & instruction assessment data as well as other key library metrics as part of a centralized assessment program Work closely with other library departments for appropriate acquisitions, cataloging, and maintenance of music materials Work closely with other library staff in support of research and instruction processes and practice Participate in professional development and scholarship Participate on library teams and initiatives as appropriate Other duties as assigned Minimum Qualifications Required: ALA-accredited MLS. Bachelor’s degree or higher in music. Strongly Preferred: Advanced degree in musicology, music theory or history, ethnomusicology, etc. Required: Instructional experience, especially in higher education Strongly Preferred: 3+ years’ experience in an academic library or equivalent, preferably in a music library Strongly Preferred: Proficiency in music cataloging techniques. Preferred: Supervisory experience, particularly in the academic library environment Preferred: Experience working with music faculty and students Preferred: Experience with copyright, music licensing, and intellectual property Expertise in music research terminology, technique and notation, including fluency with print and digital resources specific to music libraries. Engagement in the music library profession, and professional organizations. Excellent administrative skills, and ability to work well with colleagues. Excellent communication skills, and ability to work well with colleagues   The salary range for this full time exempt level position is $71,000-$77,000. Please submit a cover letter with along with your resume. Application review will begin on September 21 and will continue until the position is filled. Weekly Hours: 40 Annual Work Schedule: Application Instructions An updated resume and cover letter are required as a part of your internal application. In your cover letter, please reflect on how your professional and personal values align with the Mission of the College of the Holy Cross to form individuals of integrity and purpose who are committed to the flourishing of all. Additional Information To review our generous benefits options, please review our detailed information here or check out our Benefits at a Glance.  The College also provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities during the hiring process in compliance with law. Please contact Human Resources for questions or to request an accommodation via email at hr@holycross.edu, or by calling 508-793-3391.  The College is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer and complies with all Federal and Massachusetts laws concerning Equal Opportunity in the workplace."
    },
